  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 100 AM Friday... \n\nKEY MESSAGE 1... A coastal low is expected to bring rainfall to \ncentral NC late Saturday and Sunday, though with high uncertainty in \nthe amounts. An active weather pattern will continue next week with \nthe potential for another low pressure system close to Thanksgiving.\n\n\ufeffThe primary forecast concern in the next few days is an area of low \npressure that is expected to develop near the Bahamas, where robust \nconvection is currently ongoing. A short wave crossing the Gulf is \nthen expected to interact with the low, tracking northeast in the \nvicinity of the Carolina coast late Saturday and Sunday. As has been \nnoted in previous discussions, there is quite a bit of uncertainty \nin the track of the low, from completely offshore to more north into \nthe Piedmont.  However there has been a relatively consistent and \nslow westward shift of the potential precipitation shield, due in \npart to interaction with an approaching of a trough that will \nultimately pick the system up and send it off through the Mid-\nAtlantic region by Monday.\n\nNHC is monitoring the system as models have suggested it could have \nsome warm core tropical characteristics initially before \ntransitioning to an extra-tropical system. Multiple model runs, and \nespecially the GFS, have seemed to suffer from some feedback issues, \nand so until a low begins to form and models can better project how \nit interacts with mid-latitude energy, the forecast uncertainty will \nlikely remain somewhat high.\n\nAs of this evening, all operational model runs, including the GFS \nand ECWMF and their respective AI-based runs, bring at least half of \nan inch of rain into the Piedmont. The official forecast has \nincreased to generally 0.75\"-1.5\" across most of the CWA, but \nensemble output suggest there is still a wide range from a couple \ntenths of an inch (25th percentile) to up to 2.5 inches (75th \npercentile). With these higher values, there is Marginal Risk for \nflash flooding eastern NC. However, this along with any chance of \nsevere weather will be depending on the track, with lesser threats \nif the track remains closer to the coast.\n\nA strong 1035+ mb surface high is then expected to migrate across \nthe Great Lakes and bring a shot of much cooler and below normal \ntemperatures through the middle of next week, followed by the \npotential for another low pressure system crossing southeastern US \ncloser to Thanksgiving.",
